 to see  more of this and there's one other  significant thing here it was an  American company that was attacked in  France and in Tunisia was a tourist  hotel and for Americans who are  traveling overseas what that's going to  mean is they have to be more and more  careful that they may be at risk in  third countries and that's part of what  we need to start thinking about also  need to start thinking about Isis in  Kuwait tell us about that well the  attack there of course was on a Shia  religious facility and it's very similar  to what we saw in the last couple of  months in Saudi Arabia and there the  agenda is a little different I think  they're the of Isis leadership wants to  provoke a fight with the Shia and create  basically a sectarian war we actually  saw some of that going back almost 10  years ago when Zarqawi who headed the  al-qaeda group interacted the same thing  he attacked Shia institutions and  mosques as part of a way of a striking  against what he viewed as an apostate  group so we see these terrorists  operating on multiple fronts focusing on  Westerners including Americans but also  focusing on the internal battle between  Sunni and Shia factions within Iraq and  and other parts of the Middle East  Michael how likely are we going to see  you know taking the example of what  happened in Tunisia for example I mean  how likely could we see that happen here  on US soil well obviously we've seen a  number of attempted attacks in the u.s.  we've seen other tax disrupted by the  way the notion of attacking a hotel  where they're tourists is something we  saw back in in Mumbai in 2008 right so I  do think we're going to see soft targets  increasingly being targeted by the  current generation of terrorists I'm  that's not to say they won't try another  big attack like 911 but as we've  degraded them over time I think they're  finding it easier now to bring multiple  attacks against multiple easier targets  attacks on the United Nations  peacekeeping 